    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked solution</b></p>
<p>Show \(f(x, y)={@f_xy@}\) is differentiable using the following definition</p>
<p><b>Definition: </b> Multivariable Differentiability. </p>
Let \(z=f(x, y)\) be defined on an open set \(S\) containing \((x_{0}, y_{0})\) where \(f_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0})\) and \(f_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0})\) exist.
</p><p>Let \(dz\) be the total differential of \(z\) at \((x_{0}, y_{0})\). Let \( \Delta z = f(x_{0}+dx, y_{0}+dy) - f(x_{0}, y_{0}) \), </p>
<p> and let \(E_{1}\) and \(E_{2}\) be functions of \(dx\) and \(dy\) such that </p>
<p>\[\Delta z = dz + E_{1}dx + E_{2}dy  \]</p>
<p>(a) \(f\) is differentiable at \((x_{0}, y_{0})\) if </p>
<p>\[\lim_{(dx, dy) \longrightarrow (0, 0)} E_{1} = 0 \quad \text{and } \quad \lim_{(dx, dy) \longrightarrow (0, 0)} E_{2} = 0 \]</p>
<p>(b) \(f\) is differentiable on \(S\) if \(f\) is differentiable at every point in \(S\). If \(f\) is differentiable on \(\mathbb{R}^2\), </p> 
<p>we say that \(f\) is differentiable everywhere.</p>
<hr>
<p>You begin by following these steps below.</p>
<p><b>Step 1:</b> Find \(f(x+dx,y+dy)\) and \( \Delta z \).</p>
<p>\(f(x+dx,y+dy)={@ta_1@}\) </p>
<p>\(\Delta z = f(x+dx,y+dy) - f(x, y) \), so</p>
<p>\( \Delta z = {@ta_2@}\)</p>
<p><b>Step 2:</b> Find \(f_{x}\), \(f_{y}\), \(E_{1}\) and \(E_{2}\).</p>
<p>It is straightforward to compute \(f_{x} = {@ta_3@} \) and \(f_{y} = {@ta_4@} \). Consider once more \(\Delta z\): </p>
<p>\( 
\begin{align} 
\Delta z &=& {@ta_2@} \quad \text{(now reorder)} \\
& = & f_{x} \cdot dx + f_{y} \cdot dy + E_{1} \cdot dx + E_{2} \cdot dy \\
\end{align}
\)
</p>
<p> We get, \(E_{1}={@ta_5@}\) and \(E_{2}={@ta_6@}\)</p>
<p><b>Step 3:</b> Evaluation of limits.
<p>\(\lim_{(dx, dy) \longrightarrow (0, 0)} E_{1} ={@ta_7@}\) and \(\lim_{(dx, dy) \longrightarrow (0, 0)} E_{2} ={@ta_8@} \)</p>
<p>Since this did not depend on a specific point \( (x_{0}, y_{0}) \), we can say that \(f(x, y) \) is differentiable. </p>
<p>for all pairs \((x, y)\) in \(\mathbb{R}^2\), or, equivalently, that \(f\) is differentiable everywhere.</p>]]></text>
